===Usage notes===
* Some distinguish between ''alright'' and {{m|en|all right}} by using ''alright'' to mean "fine, good, okay" and {{m|en|all right}} to mean "all correct". Alternatively (or in addition to the previous), ''Alright'' may be used as an interjection akin to "OK", while ''all right'' is used in the sense of "unharmed, healthy".
* The contracted term is considered nonstandard by ''Garner's Modern American Usage'' and ''American Heritage Dictionary''. Indeed, the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' notes that although analogous forms exist in words such as {{m|en|already}}, {{m|en|altogether}} and {{m|en|always}} (and, indeed, {{m|en|although}}), "the contracted form is strongly criticized in the vast majority of usage guides, but without cogent reasons".<ref name="OED">"all right, adv., adj., int., and n.". OED Online. September 2012. Oxford University Press. 4 October 2012 <[http://oed.com/view/Entry/5485]>.</ref> The Oxford Dictionaries also conclude that "''alright'' remains nonstandard"<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20150729211048/http://www.oxforddictionaries.com/us/definition/american_english/all-right "All right"], American English. ''Oxford Dictionaries''. Oxford University Press.</ref> and that it is "still regarded as being unacceptable in formal writing".<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20151211035414/http://www.oxforddictionaries.com/us/definition/english/all-right "All right"], British English. ''Oxford Dictionaries''. Oxford University Press.</ref> Other dictionaries and style manuals also consider it incorrect or less correct than {{m|en|all right}}.<ref>[http://www.chicagomanualofstyle.org/qanda/data/faq/topics/Usage/faq0022.html "Usage: Which one is correct: ''alright'' or ''all right''?"] Chicago Manual of Style (2015) "Dictionaries and style manuals still tend to indicate that ''alright'' is less legitimate than ''all right''."</ref>

